{
  "id": "2026-09-15-documents-retained-source-reassessment",
  "scope": "documents",
  "kind": "review",
  "date": "2026-09-15",
  "question": "Which document conversion laws should DOCX file import, export and Word paste share, and where must fidelity limits be explicit?",
  "model": "GPT-6 / Codex",
  "method": "best-api-review/history-v2; Plite Research pinned local OSS inspection; Redesign from First Principles; GitHub issue and pull-request failure-history review",
  "trigger": "The user requested a fresh documents Best API Review after broader current GitHub OSS research and asked for harsh honest feedback.",
  "requirements": [
    "Keep the adopted semantic import result, immutable projection-aware export capture, structured diagnostics, bounded package admission and WordPastePlugin boundary unless stronger evidence disproves them.",
    "Treat import, edit and export from one first-party editor as an existing user job whose unrelated admitted DOCX content should survive where correspondence can be proved.",
    "Make source retention explicit and opt-in through a typed opaque immutable disposable artifact; keep source bytes outside EditorDocumentValue, plugin state, editor metadata and generic persistence.",
    "Bind retained source to the admitted package, imported semantic baseline, installed schema fingerprint, comments, resources and review projection so wrong-document reuse is rejected.",
    "Allow exact original-byte output only for a matching unchanged source and review state; accepted and proposed projections are semantic transformations.",
    "Preserve edited source parts or XML spans only when provenance maps the Plate edit safely; otherwise invalidate, fall back or fail with existing structured DOCX diagnostics.",
    "Keep digital signatures, unsafe relationships and unreachable or stale package claims out of preservation promises and diagnose each deliberate omission.",
    "Prove package-entry deltas and relationship reachability together with semantic and review reopen behavior, browser workflow, native-office readability, memory and complete-operation time."
  ],
  "evidenceReuse": "Reused the adopted Plate implementation and its focused, package, browser, LibreOffice and benchmark receipts as the verified semantic baseline. Expanded design evidence across 14 current OSS repositories: eight were deep-read at pinned local commits, 70 GitHub issue or pull-request search hits were screened, and eight failure-history records were deep-read. OpenDoc, Scriptor, Folio, EigenPal 2.x, docx-cli, docx-redline-js, Open XML SDK and python-docx independently established source identity, package ownership, unknown-part retention, edit-aware invalidation and package-delta proof. External suites and repository fidelity claims were read but not replayed.",
  "summary": "Pursue a Task design plan for explicit retained-source preservation. This supersedes only the prior blanket deferral of source retention: Plate already exposes import Word, edit and export Word as one first-party workflow, so diagnostics alone do not satisfy preservation of unrelated source content. Keep semantic conversion as the default and keep the verified import/export, projection, diagnostics, package-limit, paste and feature-owner boundaries. The retained source must be opt-in, opaque, source-bound and invalidation-aware; whole-ZIP passthrough, hidden editor state, a second DocxSession and an OOXML canonical-model replacement are rejected.",
  "verdict": "pursue",
  "previous": "2026-09-15-documents-canonical-conversion-implementation",
  "relation": "supersedes",
  "alternatives": [
    "Keep semantic conversion only: retain as the default and as a valid explicit fresh-package choice, but reject as the complete first-party import, edit and export experience because unsupported source content is destroyed rather than preserved.",
    "Return an explicit bounded retained-source artifact from an opted-in import and pass it explicitly to export: selected for design because it adds source correspondence without creating a second document truth.",
    "Keep the original ZIP without part or XML-span provenance: reject because regenerated owned parts still lose unknown XML, relationships can become unreachable and stale source formatting can overwrite edits.",
    "Hide retained bytes in editor metadata or plugin state: reject because format-specific payload lifetime, disposal, persistence and wrong-document pairing become implicit.",
    "Create DocxSession as a second editor facade: reject because Plate already owns transactions, selection, comments, schema, persistence and feature commands; no independent native-DOCX command job earns the duplicate owner.",
    "Replace EditorDocumentValue with a typed and generic OOXML tree: reject for Plate because it would make Word vocabulary canonical and duplicate math, emoji, media, table, comment and custom-node ownership; it remains coherent for DOCX-native products.",
    "Use surgical XML mutation as every export path: reject because arbitrary configured Plate edits cannot always map to original OOXML; retain it as an internal safe-preservation technique and package-delta oracle."
  ],
  "proofLimits": "This review selects a design direction, not a final public TypeScript spelling, preservation algorithm or implementation plan. No Plate retained-source code, test, benchmark, browser path or native-office proof exists yet. The external findings are pinned source and issue-history evidence; their suites, corpora and fidelity claims were not executed independently. Scriptor corpus counts are repository-reported. EigenPal issues 56 and 70 concern version 1.9 and are failure-history evidence for its later architecture; open issue 113 has version ambiguity and is not proof against current 2.x. No Microsoft Word certification, macro execution, digital-signature preservation, arbitrary package-preserving edit, imported-media fidelity or legal reuse of external code is claimed. No commit, push, PR, release or external publication is claimed.",
